This morpheme reflects of a dialectal Middle Chinese pronunciation of {{zh-l|*關}} which had initial {{IPAfont|*l-}} and descended from {{IPAfont|*k.rˤ-}}, instead of from {{IPAfont|*kˤr-}} like {{ltc-l|關}}, whence {{m|vi|quan#Etymology 4|quan}}.

Nohara (2023)<ref>Nohara, Masaki. 2023. Old Chinese ‘egg’: More evidence for consonant clusters. In: ''Language and Linguistics'' 24:2 (2023), pp. 325–344.</ref> is an in-depth treatment into the lexeme "egg" in Old Chinese, presenting etymologically related pairs such as {{ltc-l|卵|id=1|egg}} (lateral onset, from {{IPAfont|*k.rˤ-}}) and {{ltc-l|𢺄|fish egg}} (velar onset, from {{IPAfont|*kˤr-}}) as evidence for complex onsets/consonant clusters in Old Chinese.

